WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
You came back for more, that's awesome.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.520
So let's talk about what products can do for us.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.370
How they give us value that we're willing to pay for.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:12.360
Products can do one or a combination of three things.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.775
They can do things cheaper than competitors.

00:00:14.775 --> 00:00:18.030
They can outperform competitors or they can make you

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.885
feel differently and perhaps better than competitors.

00:00:21.885 --> 00:00:26.820
So a car can give us value because it's cheaper than a competitor or it can be more fuel

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.740
efficient or maybe it's just got

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.440
a cool logo that makes us feel proud to drive it, or all three.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.730
Branding, which is a set of associations that a consumer

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.385
has about a product is an example of psychological value.

00:00:39.385 --> 00:00:42.620
A brand is meant to make a consumer feel a certain way.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.095
It doesn't change how fast the car drives,

00:00:45.095 --> 00:00:47.690
it changes how exciting that ride feels.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.210
People are willing to pay for products that are branded because

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:53.015
they value the feelings of the specific brand gives them.

00:00:53.015 --> 00:00:55.625
Some people feel that brands and branding are wrong.

00:00:55.625 --> 00:00:57.230
There was even a book called,

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:01.100
"No Logo," that criticized the ultra branded world in which we live.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.770
But if brands are wrong,

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.405
then feelings are wrong,

00:01:04.405 --> 00:01:06.140
and I don't think that anyone would argue

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.255
that there's something bad about having feelings,

00:01:08.255 --> 00:01:10.370
even about things we consume.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.865
Let's talk about an example of an unconventional brand.

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:17.360
You might not have heard the name Calvin Cordozar Broadus Jr.,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.640
but you probably are familiar with Snoop Doggy Dogg.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.720
Snoop created his brand by

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.960
capitalizing on the fact that people were interested in his music,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.075
but he didn't limit himself to music.

00:01:29.075 --> 00:01:34.645
He use his music as a platform to entice listeners to find out more about himself.

00:01:34.645 --> 00:01:37.255
Snoop Dogg made elements of himself,

00:01:37.255 --> 00:01:39.590
elements that have nothing to do with music,

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.375
available for the public to see.

00:01:41.375 --> 00:01:43.430
So he created a TV game show.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:44.975
He appeared to football games.

00:01:44.975 --> 00:01:47.450
He did a cooking special with Martha Stewart.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.815
He even created a nature show called Plizzanet Earth.

00:01:50.815 --> 00:01:54.590
Essentially, he helped fans generate associations and feelings about him,

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.770
and not simply his rap and its lyrics.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:00.050
Through marketing, Snoop was able to move from being

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:04.445
just a voice in people's minds to a personality that they care to discover.

00:02:04.445 --> 00:02:08.990
He licensed his likeness and use PR to generate press and TV appearances.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:13.360
In addition, his ubiquity included participation in other musicians' work.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.760
Even more mainstream pop like Katy Perry and Miley Cyrus,

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:17.780
the Pussy Cat Dolls,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.610
Mariah Carey, and some hip hop like Matoma.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:24.050
Here's the thing about psychological value that's so important.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.250
Psychological value provides a form of differentiation that consumers care

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.800
about and create reasons to buy

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.100
something when a product is otherwise the same functionally.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.370
Think of a car. All cars have engines,

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.155
many of the same size and power,

00:02:39.155 --> 00:02:41.630
and many features are exactly the same.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.300
So what makes you buy a BMW over an Audi or

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.270
Mercedes when their performance and price are very similar.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:51.065
The big differences are in the associations we have with each brand.

00:02:51.065 --> 00:02:54.125
Those associations are pure psychological value.

00:02:54.125 --> 00:02:57.380
When BMW calls itself the Ultimate Driving Machine,

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:01.490
it's appealing to one element in our psychology that is different from Audis,

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:03.345
which says, "never blend in,"

00:03:03.345 --> 00:03:05.090
kind of like Snoop Dogg.

